## Distributional Partial Derivatives of the First-Quadrant Characteristic Function

Let $f = \mathbf{1}_{\{x_1>0,\,x_2>0\}}$ be the characteristic function of the open first quadrant $Q$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$. We compute its distributional partial derivatives by duality with test functions $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^2)$.

### First-order derivatives

**$\partial_{x_1} f$.** By definition of the distributional derivative,
$$\langle \partial_{x_1} f,\, \varphi\rangle = -\langle f,\, \partial_{x_1}\varphi\rangle = -\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\!\int_{0}^{\infty} \partial_{x_1}\varphi(x_1,x_2)\,dx_1\,dx_2.$$

Integrating in $x_1$ (with $\varphi$ compactly supported, so the boundary term at $+\infty$ vanishes):
$$= -\int_{0}^{\infty}\Big[\varphi(x_1,x_2)\Big]_{x_1=0}^{x_1\to\infty} dx_2 = \int_{0}^{\infty} \varphi(0,x_2)\,dx_2.$$

This is exactly the action of the distribution $\delta(x_1)\otimes \mathbf{1}_{\{x_2>0\}}$ (a Dirac mass in $x_1$ at $0$, supported on the open positive $x_2$-half-axis). Hence
$$\boxed{\partial_{x_1} f = \delta(x_1)\otimes \mathbf{1}_{\{x_2>0\}}.}$$

**$\partial_{x_2} f$.** By symmetry,
$$\langle \partial_{x_2} f,\, \varphi\rangle = -\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\!\int_{0}^{\infty}\partial_{x_2}\varphi\,dx_1\,dx_2 = \int_{0}^{\infty}\varphi(x_1,0)\,dx_1,$$
so
$$\boxed{\partial_{x_2} f = \mathbf{1}_{\{x_1>0\}}\otimes \delta(x_2).}$$

### Second-order mixed derivative

**$\partial_{x_1}\partial_{x_2} f$.** Apply $\partial_{x_1}$ to $\partial_{x_2} f = \mathbf{1}_{\{x_1>0\}}\otimes\delta(x_2)$:
$$\langle \partial_{x_1}\partial_{x_2} f,\,\varphi\rangle = -\langle \mathbf{1}_{\{x_1>0\}}\otimes\delta(x_2),\,\partial_{x_1}\varphi\rangle = -\int_{0}^{\infty}\partial_{x_1}\varphi(x_1,0)\,dx_1 = \varphi(0,0).$$

This is the Dirac delta at the origin:
$$\boxed{\partial_{x_1}\partial_{x_2} f = \partial_{x_2}\partial_{x_1} f = \delta_{(0,0)}.}$$

### Pure second derivatives

**$\partial_{x_1}^2 f$.** Differentiate $\partial_{x_1} f = \delta(x_1)\otimes\mathbf{1}_{\{x_2>0\}}$:
$$\langle \partial_{x_1}^2 f,\,\varphi\rangle = -\langle \delta(x_1)\otimes\mathbf{1}_{\{x_2>0\}},\,\partial_{x_1}\varphi\rangle = -\int_{0}^{\infty}\partial_{x_1}\varphi(0,x_2)\,dx_2.$$

This is $\delta'(x_1)\otimes\mathbf{1}_{\{x_2>0\}}$ (the distributional derivative of $\delta$ in $x_1$, tensored with $\mathbf{1}_{\{x_2>0\}}$):
$$\boxed{\partial_{x_1}^2 f = \delta'(x_1)\otimes\mathbf{1}_{\{x_2>0\}}.}$$

Similarly,
$$\boxed{\partial_{x_2}^2 f = \mathbf{1}_{\{x_1>0\}}\otimes\delta'(x_2).}$$

### Summary

Writing $H$ for the Heaviside function and $\delta$ for the Dirac distribution, with $f = H(x_1)H(x_2)$:

| Derivative | Result |
|---|---|
| $\partial_{x_1} f$ | $\delta(x_1)\,H(x_2)$ |
| $\partial_{x_2} f$ | $H(x_1)\,\delta(x_2)$ |
| $\partial_{x_1}\partial_{x_2} f$ | $\delta(x_1)\,\delta(x_2) = \delta_{(0,0)}$ |
| $\partial_{x_1}^2 f$ | $\delta'(x_1)\,H(x_2)$ |
| $\partial_{x_2}^2 f$ | $H(x_1)\,\delta'(x_2)$ |

The key structural point: the first-order derivatives concentrate the jump of $f$ across each coordinate axis onto a Dirac mass along that axis (restricted to the half-line where the other coordinate keeps $f=1$), and the mixed second derivative collapses both jumps to a point mass at the origin.

### PROOF COMPLETE
